<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- MAS flavor: sidecar binary (clauge-server) entitlements. Applied via
         post-build codesign re-sign in scripts/build-mas-clean.sh.
         Tauri 2 signs every binary in the bundle with the same entitlements
         file by default. After commit db51ce5 added application-identifier +
         team-identifier to entitlements.mas.plist, the sidecar inherited them
         too — but the sidecar has no per-binary provisioning profile, so
         Transporter 90885 (nested executable profile mismatch) fires.
         Standard MAS practice: sign helpers/sidecars with minimal entitlements
         (no app-id, no team-id) so they don't trip nested-executable profile
         checks. The main bundle keeps the full entitlements + embedded profile.

         v0.9.10 (Apple Issue 2 fix): com.apple.security.app-sandbox is
         REMOVED from this list. clauge-server is a standalone Mach-O
         binary (no .app bundle wrapper) and therefore has NO embedded
         Info.plist with `CFBundleIdentifier`. When the binary launches
         WITH the app-sandbox entitlement, libsystem_secinit.dylib's
         `_libsecinit_appsandbox` tries to set up a per-bundle sandbox
         container, fails to find a bundle identifier via
         `kCFBundleIdentifierKey`, and the binary SIGTRAPs during dyld
         initializer execution — BEFORE any of our Rust/Node code runs.
         This was the actual root cause of Apple's v0.9.0 build 3 rejection
         under Guideline 2.1(a) ("app does not load its content after
         launch"); the wizard race fix is necessary but not sufficient.

         Without the app-sandbox entitlement, secinitd skips per-bundle
         setup for this binary and the kernel applies the parent's sandbox
         via process-tree inheritance. Verified locally 2026-05-28: sidecar
         spawned, bound 127.0.0.1:3456, /api/health returned with
         claudeDir resolved to the parent's container path
         (~/Library/Containers/com.clauding.clauge/Data/.claude). The
         security posture is unchanged — the sidecar is still sandboxed,
         just inheriting rather than declaring its own container.

         The remaining keys are runtime-hardening / non-sandbox flags:
         cs.allow-jit is required for V8 JIT in the SEA Node binary, and
         the network + files entitlements are kept for defense-in-depth
         (harmless under inherited sandbox; would matter if Apple later
         requires per-binary entitlement declarations). -->
    <!-- network.client: outbound HTTPS to api.anthropic.com (the actual caller). -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- network.server: listens on 127.0.0.1:3456 for the Tauri main process. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- cs.allow-jit: required for the SEA Node binary's V8 JIT. NOT a sandbox
         entitlement — it's a code-signing hardening flag. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- files.user-selected.read-only: harmless under inherited sandbox. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-only</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- files.bookmarks.app-scope: harmless under inherited sandbox. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
